### single node, 10 Cats, LOCAL file object store (same NVMe as the cache)
label,phase,requested,completed,min_ms,p50_ms,p95_ms,max_ms,failures
single-10-file,create,10,10,2572,12220,24277,24277,0
# settled: 10/10 observed=running after 24s

Reading: with the file adapter, 10 concurrent creates settle in 24.3 s, which is
10x the 2.4 s single-create time -- exactly what strict per-node serialization
predicts (NodeRuntime::execute holds a node-wide mutex for everything except
ResumeCat). Switching the durable layer to S3 adds 5.3x on top, because every
create seeds and uploads the 512 MiB golden root. The two-node run is NOT clean
evidence about cluster scaling: teardown of the previous 20-Cat batch may have
overlapped it.
